---
/*
  Analysis piece 01: the crawl of 3,928 small business websites.

  This is the piece nobody else can write, because nobody else has the dataset.
  Every other agency quoting website statistics is quoting somebody else's
  survey. Source of every figure here: docs/crawl-findings-2026-08-21.md, which
  was recounted over the full unfiltered crawl on 21 August 2026.

  Read that file before changing a number on this page. An earlier set of
  figures from the same dataset was computed over the filtered outreach list and
  overstated the headline by twelve percentage points: it would have said 55% of
  small business sites have no HTTPS when the honest figure is 42.9%. That
  mistake is the reason the findings file exists, and it is exactly the kind of
  number a sceptical reader checks first.

  Two rules this page cannot break:

  1. Every figure carries its own denominator and its date, inline, so no claim
     can be lifted out and made to say something else. Defect rates are a share
     of the 2,828 sites that loaded. The "no website" figure is a share of the
     3,928 businesses. They are not interchangeable.
  2. All six caveats appear near the top, in the method callout, not buried at
     the bottom. Including the awkward one: 474 sites were unreachable and are
     excluded, so the true "no working website" figure is higher than 15.9%,
     not lower.

  The charts are computed from the counts, not drawn by hand. If a figure
  changes, regenerate rather than nudging a coordinate.
*/
import AnalysisLayout from "../../layouts/AnalysisLayout.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/analysis/SectionHead.astro";
import StatTiles from "../../components/analysis/StatTiles.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/analysis/Figure.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/analysis/Callout.astro";

const tiles = [
    {
        who: "No HTTPS",
        big: "42.9%",
        sub: "Of the 2,828 sites that loaded. Chrome shows a \"Not secure\" warning before a visitor sees a price.",
        series: 1 as const,
    },
    {
        who: "Passed all eight checks",
        big: "15.6%",
        sub: "442 of 2,828. Passing is a floor, not a standard.",
        series: 3 as const,
    },
    {
        who: "No website at all",
        big: "15.9%",
        sub: "Of the 3,928 businesses listed on Google Maps. In the US it was one in four.",
        series: 2 as const,
    },
];

const REGION_LEGEND = [
    { label: "UK (n = 1,301)", series: 1 as const },
    { label: "Gulf (n = 760)", series: 2 as const },
    { label: "US (n = 305)", series: 3 as const },
];
---

<AnalysisLayout
    title="We crawled 2,828 small business websites. 42.9% of them still had no HTTPS."
    seoTitle="What 2,828 small business sites got wrong"
    description="A crawl of 3,928 businesses listed on Google Maps across the UK, the Gulf and the US in August 2026. What their websites actually do, with the denominators attached."
    eyebrow="Original research &middot; 3,928 businesses &middot; August 2026"
    lede="We went looking for customers and ended up with a dataset. In August 2026 we crawled the websites of 3,928 businesses listed on Google Maps across the UK, the Gulf and the US, and ran eight basic checks on each homepage. Of the 2,828 sites that loaded, 42.9% still had no HTTPS, and only 15.6% passed all eight."
    published="2026-08-25"
    dataSource="Quadem crawl of 3,928 businesses, August 2026"
>

    <section>
        <div class="analysis-col">
            <SectionHead num="01" title="The headline" />
            <p>Of 2,828 small business websites we crawled across the UK, the Gulf and the US in August 2026, <strong>42.9% still had no HTTPS</strong>, meaning Chrome shows a "Not secure" warning before a visitor sees a price.</p>
            <p><strong>Only 15.6% passed all eight of our basic checks.</strong></p>
            <p>And before any of that: <strong>15.9% of the 3,928 businesses we found on Google Maps had no website listed at all.</strong> In the US that rose to one in four.</p>
        </div>

        <StatTiles tiles={tiles} />
    </section>

    <section>
        <div class="analysis-col">
            <SectionHead num="02" title="What this is, and what it is not" />
            <p>The numbers below are worth exactly as much as the method behind them, so the method comes first rather than last.</p>
            <Callout label="Method, and its limits">
                <p><strong>It is not a random sample.</strong> Businesses came from Google Maps searches for chosen service categories in chosen cities. It represents the kind of business we sell to, not small businesses in general.</p>
                <p><strong>Homepage only.</strong> Every check ran against the homepage. A site can pass all eight and still be poor on every other page.</p>
                <p><strong>474 sites were unreachable</strong> when we crawled them and are excluded from the defect rates. Some of those are dead sites, which means the true figure for businesses with no working website is <em>higher</em> than 15.9%, not lower.</p>
                <p><strong>"No website listed" means no website on the Google Maps listing.</strong> A business might have one it has not linked.</p>
                <p><strong>Eight checks, and they are basic ones:</strong> a secure connection, a page title, a title short enough not to be cut off in Google, a meta description, a mobile viewport tag, a social share image, a link to Instagram or TikTok, and a homepage that is not oversized. Passing all eight is a floor, not a standard.</p>
                <p><strong>Crawled August 2026.</strong> Every figure on this page carries that date.</p>
            </Callout>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <div class="analysis-col">
            <SectionHead num="03" title="Where the 3,928 went" />
            <p>Two different denominators run through this piece and they are not interchangeable, so here is the split before any percentage appears.</p>
        </div>

