    <section>
        <div class="analysis-col">
            <SectionHead num="06" title="Six things you can check on your own site in ten minutes" />
            <p>These are the same checks, run by hand. None of them need a tool or a consultant.</p>
            <ol class="checks">
                <li><strong>Look at your own address bar on a phone.</strong> If it says "Not secure" instead of showing a padlock, you are in the 42.9%. Most hosts now issue a certificate free, and it is usually a setting rather than a project.</li>
                <li><strong>Paste your homepage address into a WhatsApp message to yourself.</strong> If the preview is a grey box with no picture, you have no social share image, and so does 42.5% of this sample. Every link anyone ever shares of your site looks broken.</li>
                <li><strong>Search Google for your business name and read your own result.</strong> If the title is cut off mid-word, it is too long. If the grey description underneath is a scrap of your navigation menu, you have no meta description and Google is guessing.</li>
                <li><strong>Check your Google Maps listing has your website on it.</strong> One in six of the businesses we found had no website linked there at all. If yours exists and is not on the listing, that is a five-minute fix worth more than most redesigns.</li>
                <li><strong>Open your site on mobile data with the office wifi turned off.</strong> This is where an oversized homepage shows up, and 12.4% of the sample had one.</li>
                <li><strong>Ask whether someone could find your Instagram from your website.</strong> Just under a third of these sites had no link to Instagram or TikTok anywhere on the homepage, which usually means the two are being run by different people who do not talk.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <div class="analysis-col">
            <SectionHead num="07" title="Why we had this data at all" />
            <p>We did not set out to write a study. We built a machine for finding businesses that need calling, because that is how a one-person studio finds work without a sales team. It goes through public listings at scale, checks each website for the defects above, and writes one line explaining why that business is worth a conversation now.</p>
            <p>The dataset is a by-product of running it. That is the honest account of where these numbers came from, and it seems better to say so than to present the crawl as research that happened for its own sake.</p>
            {/* The other half of the link the Fieldwork copy asks for in both
                directions. It could not be added when this page was written,
                because Fieldwork was noindex and disallowed until 28 August 2026. */}
            <p>That machine is now sold as a service, pointed at one client's definition of their own customer rather than at a whole market. It is called <a href="/services/fieldwork/">Fieldwork</a>.</p>

            <div class="foot">
                <p><strong>Who wrote this.</strong> Ernest, Quadem Digital, a founder-led studio in Accra building websites, brand and video. The full counts, the regional breakdown and the measured selection bias are in our working notes, and I will send them to anyone who wants to check the arithmetic.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

</AnalysisLayout>
